# UI/UX — tokens by intent

`context/instructions/ui-tokens.md` is the reference for the values that make a
look consistent: colour, spacing, type, radius and motion. It is **conditional**:
it applies only when the project already has a token layer or design system. Read
it with `context/instructions/ui.md` (the doctrine) and
`context/instructions/ui-adapters.md` (how tokens map to a framework's theming).

## When this applies (no-op rule)

- Use it when the project has tokens or a design system to extend. If it has
  none, this file does not apply: **stop and ask** — do not create a design
  system, and do not introduce one as a side effect of a change.
- The project's own system is the source of truth. Local rules belong in the
  AGENTS.md project block, not here.

## Tokens by intent, not by appearance

- Name a token for the role it plays, not the value it holds:
  `action.primary`, `surface.raised`, `text.muted` — never `blue-500` or
  `gray-3`.
- A system may have three tiers, used in one direction:
  **primitive** (raw values) → **semantic** (roles and intent) → **component**
  (component-specific). Components reference semantic or component tokens; they
  never reference primitives or raw values.
- The intent name is what makes a theme swap and a redesign possible without
  touching every component.

## Naming

Use one shape per project: `{category}.{property}.{variant}-{state}` — for
example `action.destructive-default`, `surface.raised-hover`. The name encodes
role, variant and state; the value never appears in the name.

## One source per project

- Keep a single token source. Do not let the same value be defined in two
  places; a token that drifts is worse than no token.
- If a value is needed that no token expresses, add the token at the right tier —
  do not inline the value.

## Theme swap at the semantic layer

- Themes (dark/light, a brand variant) swap **semantic** values. Primitives stay
  stable; components are unaware of the theme.
- Never branch component code on the theme; express the difference as a token
  value.
- Check both themes when a component uses a new token.

## Extraction threshold

- Extract a token only for a value that recurs **three or more times with the same
  intent**. Premature abstraction is worse than duplication.
- If no system exists, do not infer one from a few values: stop and ask (see the
  no-op rule).

## Boundary

- Choosing or adopting a design system is a library-first decision
  (`context/instructions/development.md`): propose a shortlist, get the user's
  choice, record it in the project block.
- How tokens become a framework's theming primitive is the adapter contract
  (`context/instructions/ui-adapters.md`).
- State enumeration and component structure live in
  `context/instructions/ui-components.md`.
